{
  "dom_id": "step-q338364",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14245914/assets/94027c25ef78ae46d0deb4b577339daf878efd71b71029a9575f9c8ed9096e2c.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of the function $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\,(f(x)-2)^{3}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=3.$\n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\,(f(x)-2)^{3}={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)-2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(2-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)-2)^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)-2))}^{3} \\\\  & =(3-2)^{3} \\\\  & ={1}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\,(f(x)-2)^{3} & =(1)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
